{"fact_key": "IN:tax-residency-182-day-rule-and-rnor-to-ror-transition", "scope": "country", "country_id": "IN", "fact_label": "Tax residency — 182-day rule and RNOR-to-ROR transition", "type": "threshold", "value_raw": "Resident if present 182+ days/year (or 60+ days current year + 365+ days across preceding 4 years). A Resident is further RNOR (India-sourced income only) if an NR in 9 of the preceding 10 tax years, or present ≤729 days across the preceding 7 tax years — typically the first 2-3 tax years of continuous stay. Once neither RNOR condition holds, the person becomes ROR and India taxes worldwide income, including foreign-client income never remitted to India.", "criterion_id": "income-viability", "model_target": "HardConstraintRule", "divergence_flag": "Not yet checked", "source_ref": "visa-legal.md", "source_url": "https://taxsummaries.pwc.com/india/individual/residence", "source_type": "professional-advisory-content", "date": "2026-08-23", "confidence": "Medium", "source_count": "single", "trajectory": "not yet assessed", "verification_state": "archived"}
{"fact_key": "IN:dtaa-double-taxation-relief-network", "scope": "country", "country_id": "IN", "fact_label": "DTAA double-taxation relief network", "type": "descriptive", "value_raw": "India has 94 comprehensive Double Taxation Avoidance Agreements (DTAAs) plus 8 limited-scope treaties, using credit and exemption methods to prevent double taxation of the same income; a unilateral Section 91 relief mechanism covers residents whose home country has no DTAA with India at all. Claiming relief requires filing/documentation with Indian tax authorities, not automatic.", "criterion_id": "income-viability", "model_target": "descriptive", "divergence_flag": "N/A", "source_ref": "visa-legal.md", "source_url": "https://www.india-briefing.com/doing-business-guide/india/why-india/india-s-double-taxation-avoidance-agreements", "source_type": "professional-advisory-content", "date": "2026-08-23", "confidence": "Medium", "source_count": "single", "trajectory": "not yet assessed", "verification_state": "archived"}
{"fact_key": "IN:individual-income-tax-slab-rates-new-regime-fy2026-27", "scope": "country", "country_id": "IN", "fact_label": "Individual income tax slab rates — new regime, FY2026-27", "type": "continuum", "value_raw": "0% up to ₹4 lakh; 5% ₹4-8 lakh; 10% ₹8-12 lakh; 15% ₹12-16 lakh; 20% ₹16-20 lakh; 25% ₹20-24 lakh; 30% above ₹24 lakh. Section 156 of the Income Tax Act, 2025 (successor provision to Section 87A under the repealed 1961 Act) rebate zeroes tax liability up to ₹12 lakh taxable income under the new regime (₹5 lakh under the old regime).", "unit": "₹ lakh/year (top marginal rate 30% above ₹24 lakh, ≈$27,000-28,500 depending on exchange rate)", "criterion_id": "income-viability", "model_target": "Criterion", "divergence_flag": "N/A", "source_ref": "visa-legal.md", "source_url": "https://www.bajajhousingfinance.in/income-tax-slab", "source_type": "aggregator-or-directory-data", "date": "2026-08-24", "confidence": "High", "source_count": "cross-corroborated", "trajectory": "stable", "verification_state": "archived"}
